Verdict: challenge. The format rule exists, but all reader boundaries do not implement its predicate.

1. Unchecked legacy pins still grant reliance. A live backed claim accepted by the fixture claims-checker with verdict accept and a full commit id, without files (the legacy form), is covered by warrant.drift.covered. drift.not_checked, agent handoff inspect, and the JS reader all report PINS_NOT_CHECKED. Python warrant.verify.verify, python -m warrant verify, and warrant_verify.py instead report lineage.reliance ELIGIBLE, pins NONE, no why_not, rely true; both commands exit 0 without any checkout. The verifier skips the acceptance at warrant/verify.py:352-354 because files is None. Its independent pin predicate therefore disagrees with drift.pinned (warrant/drift.py:73-87), which explicitly includes the checker's earlier acceptances even without files. This directly leaves D's commit-pin case unfinished. These are deterministic test identities, not repository actor keys. This is not a claim that M's own store currently contains such a legacy acceptance.

2. Python verification does not pass the complete shared vector. Ask for every lineage in tests/vectors/pins-not-checked.json. The retired claim 6d1f302603cf7ed2f9d8cd0c7199ebaf22d22b1bd80c1f6870d05fdd547428d3 is expected ELIGIBLE (outside drift.covered) but Python verify and the standalone verifier show PINS_NOT_CHECKED. JavaScript matches the vector. verify._root at warrant/verify.py:390-417 applies _pins without the covered/retired predicate. VP1 at tests/verify_test.py:1154-1155 and 1170 selects only expected PINS_NOT_CHECKED roots, so it cannot detect this vector mismatch.

Expected repair: make no-checkout Python verification use the same covered/pinned predicate as the other readers (including legacy checker acceptances and the retired-claim exception), for the effective version whichever version is asked. Hold all reader projections to every row of the vector and add the legacy case, with a mutant. Generic nonchecker acceptances with no files must keep their existing unpinned behavior.

